    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/index.ts",
    "line": 211,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Every error in a group move is reported as \"stale\"**\n\nThe `catch` around `workspaceGate().run(...)` turns any thrown error into `refuseAll(\"stale\")`. Only a `StaleWorkspaceError` from the gate means that. Two other errors also land here:\n\n- `vaultActivity.run(\"library\", …)` calls `admit()`, which throws `MaintenanceError` (`vault-activity.ts:96`) when \"Check and fix files\" starts after the early `inMaintenance` check at line 161 but before the gate admits the call.\n- A rejected `loadDeviceId` throws too.\n\nIn both cases the user is told their window's workspace is no longer active, which is false.\n\nSuggested fix: map `StaleWorkspaceError` to \"stale\" and `MaintenanceError` to \"maintenance\". Map anything else to \"io\". This is the same mapping `runMoveGroup` already uses for an item that throws.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in fac69179: a thrown group move now maps `StaleWorkspaceError` to `stale`, `MaintenanceError` to `maintenance` and anything else to `io` (`refusalOfError`, with a unit test)."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769214,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/index.ts",
    "line": 171,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A rejected device-id promise is cached for the rest of the process**\n\n`deviceId ??= loadDeviceId(...)` keeps the promise even when it rejects. After one failure, for example a transient read error in userData, every later group move rejects at `await deviceId` until the app restarts. With the catch below, the user also sees each of those as \"stale\". Consider resetting `deviceId = null` when the promise rejects.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in fac69179: a rejected `loadDeviceId` clears the cached promise, so the next group asks again."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769220,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/shared/types/workspaces.ts",
    "line": 208,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠📝 **Fix · docs: `skipped` doc says whole-group refusals go there, but the code puts them in `failed`**\n\nThe comment on `skipped` says it also holds items from a group that \"was refused before it began\". But `moveItems` in `main/workspaces/index.ts:153-158` builds a whole-group refusal (`busy`, `stale`, `maintenance`) with every item in `failed` and `skipped: []`. Only Stop fills `skipped` (`move-group.ts:38`). Either change the comment, or have `refuseAll` fill `skipped` if that is the intended contract.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in fac69179: the comment on `skipped` now says only Stop fills it; a group refused as a whole lists every item in `failed` and leaves `skipped` empty. I kept the code's contract, because the renderer already counts `failed` for that case."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769227,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/library/tag-repository.ts",
    "line": 253,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: ensureByName can create tags that the result then drops**\n\nMissing tags are minted and written for every entry in `wanted`, but the result is cut to `TAGS_PER_ITEM_MAX` afterwards. If the input has more names than the per-item cap, the extra tags stay in the destination registry without belonging to any item. Consider stopping once `result.length` reaches `TAGS_PER_ITEM_MAX`, before minting more.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in fac69179: `ensureByName` stops before minting once the result reaches `TAGS_PER_ITEM_MAX`. A test checks the registry holds exactly 20 tags for 25 names."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769236,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/move-item.ts",
    "line": 674,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Roll-forward deletes every item's drag folders, not just this item's**\n\nWhen `healEntry` rolls a move forward, it runs `rm(join(sourceDir, META_DIR, \"drag\"), { recursive: true })`. That deletes the whole `.kaipu/drag/` folder, so drag copies of every item in the source workspace go with it. Some of those may have been dragged less than `DRAG_SETTLE_MS` ago, so a drop target can still be reading them. The comment says \"a drag copy of the item\", and `DragLedger.clear` (drag-ledger.ts:38-45) only removes the folders of the one item, in line with decision 11.\n\nThis also runs while the app is open, not only on startup. `healMoves` runs before each move (index.ts:117), and `moveLocked`'s post-commit catch calls `healEntry` directly (line 409). So an unrelated drag that is still in progress can lose its file.\n\nSuggested fix: limit the cleanup to this item's folders. Use the ledger's entries when they exist, and on startup the folders whose names identify the item. If the folders cannot be told apart by item, skip folders younger than `DRAG_SETTLE_MS`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in 3639b994: confirmed. Healing runs before every group and after a failed commit, and the drag folders are named by uuid, so it cannot tell which belong to the item. It no longer touches `.kaipu/drag/` (startup and quit already sweep it; the move itself clears the item's folders through the ledger). A test pins that another item's drag copy survives a heal. ADR 0033 decision 11 is updated in 670b1ba7."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769241,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/move-item.ts",
    "line": 416,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A move that already committed is reported as `io` if post-commit healing throws**\n\nOnce `mediaPlaced` is true, the target holds the item. But if the `healEntry` call here throws (for example, `writeJournal` or `readSidecarStrict` fail on an I/O error), the error goes to `moveItem`'s catch at line 204, and the move returns `refuse(\"io\")`. The renderer then says the item failed to move when it is already in the target, and possibly half-removed from the source.\n\nSuggested fix: wrap this `healEntry` call in its own try/catch, log the error, leave the journal for `healMoves` to finish, and still return `{ ok: true, newId }`.\n\nAlso, line 414 always reports `mode: \"copied\"`, even after a hard link. The real `mode` is in scope and could be returned instead.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in 3639b994: the post-commit `healEntry` is wrapped, so a failing heal is logged, the journal stays for the next run, and the move still returns `ok: true`. It also returns the real `mode` now (`linked` after a hard link). A test makes the journal folder unwritable after the commit and checks both."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769246,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/move/item-move-menu.tsx",
    "line": 92,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Pressing Enter or Space on a \"Move to\" target opens the item instead of moving it**\n\nThe menu is portaled to `document.body`, but React portals still pass events up the React tree. The wrappers here (line 49 and line 82) stop only `click`. A `keydown` on a focused target `<button role=\"menuitem\">` in `MoveTargetList` therefore reaches the card's `onKeyDown` (`video-card.tsx:81-86`) or the row's (`video-row.tsx:68-73`). That handler checks for Enter or Space without looking at `e.target`. It calls `e.preventDefault()`, which stops the button's synthesized click, then `activate()` → `onNavigate()`. So a keyboard user who opens the menu and presses Enter on a workspace navigates into the item and never moves it. In select mode the same keypress toggles the item's checkbox instead. The \"⋯\" button has the same problem: Enter on it opens the item rather than the menu.\n\nFix: also stop `onKeyDown` propagation on the anchor div and the portal wrapper. Another option is to have the card and row handlers ignore events whose `target !== currentTarget`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in 7e503dfa: confirmed. The card and row now ignore a key that did not start on themselves (`e.target !== e.currentTarget`), which covers the portaled menu items and the ⋯ button. Tests press Enter and Space on a control inside the card and the row."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769251,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/move/move-flow.ts",
    "line": 101,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Stop before anything moved reports \"Moved 0 items\"**\n\nWhen the user presses Stop before the first move finishes, the result can have `moved: []`, `failed: []` and `skipped: [...]`. That state misses the first branch (line 85, because skipped > 0) and the `resultNone` branch (line 87, because failed is 0), so it lands in this `else` branch. The toast then reads `resultAll` with `count: 0`, \"Moved 0 items to Personal\", followed by the skipped count. Add a branch for `moved.length === 0` that says nothing moved.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in bbe5d284: a result with nothing moved and nothing failed (Stop before the first move) now takes the `resultNone` branch, followed by the not-started count, instead of 'Moved 0 items'. Test added."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769256,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/move/move-flow.ts",
    "line": 60,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: orderForMove puts each recording's exports in reverse order**\n\nThe loop walks `rest` backwards and pushes each match as it splices it out. That reverses the exports relative to the loaded list: the test at move-flow.test.ts:43 expects `rec, exp2, exp1`. `MoveRequest.items` is documented as \"in the order on screen\", and with this ordering the group moves the exports, and reports progress on them, in reverse. You could collect the matches in a forward pass and then remove them from `rest`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in bbe5d284: `orderForMove` collects each recording's exports in a forward pass, so they keep the on-screen order. The unit test and the integration test expected the reversed order; both now expect the on-screen one."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769265,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/move/use-move-items.ts",
    "line": 93,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: An IPC error is reported to the user as \"stale\"**\n\nIf `moveItems` rejects, every item is marked failed with reason `stale`. The toast then gives the `reason_stale` text for each item, which is not what happened. The real cause is only written to the console. A generic error message, or a dedicated reason, would describe the failure correctly.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in bbe5d284: a rejected `moveItems` is now reported with reason `io` (\"a file couldn't be copied; the original is untouched\"), not `stale`. Test added."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438769272,
    "path": "packages/i18n/messages/es.json",
    "line": 2623,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Spanish move strings say \"workspace\" where the rest of the app says \"espacio de trabajo\"**\n\nThree new `move.*` strings leave the term in English: `chipLabel` (\"Mover a otro workspace\", line 2617), `newWorkspace` (\"Nuevo workspace…\", line 2623) and `reason_stale` (\"el workspace cambió\", line 2662). The existing Spanish strings for the same idea say \"espacio de trabajo\": `add` is \"Nuevo espacio de trabajo…\" (line 2562), and so are `addAction` and `newTitle` (lines 2588–2589), and `editNotWritable` uses it too. As a result, the switcher shows \"Nuevo espacio de trabajo…\" and the Move to menu shows \"Nuevo workspace…\" for the same action. Fix: use \"espacio de trabajo\" in all three strings.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in 180fd5d8: `chipLabel`, `newWorkspace` and `reason_stale` say \"espacio de trabajo\". (`locationParentMissing` still quotes the \"Default workspace\" name; that is the workspace's actual name, not the term.)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438859073,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/move/use-move-items.ts",
    "line": 93,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: An IPC failure now shows the \"io\" text, which says the original was not touched**\n\nWhen `moveItems` rejects, each item is now marked `io`. That is better than `stale`. But `reason_io` reads \"couldn't copy a file; the original was untouched\" (es.json:2663: \"no se pudo copiar un archivo; el original no se tocó\"). The renderer can't know that. The main process may have run some or all of the group before the IPC reply was lost. In that case the toast says \"Nothing moved\" for items that are already in the target. Consider a dedicated reason (for example `unknown`, worded \"the result could not be read; check both workspaces\"), or refetch the library before reporting.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in 02dbb2ec: a rejected `moveItems` no longer builds per-item results. The toast now says \"The result of the move could not be read. Check both workspaces.\" (new `move.resultUnknown`, en and es), because main may have moved part of the group before the reply was lost. Test updated."
      }
    ]
  }
]

